From: kevin03L <kevin03L@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Tegra210: correct H.264 and MPEG-2, fast read back, no leaks

Decoding:
- planes are 16Bx2 block linear with two GOBs: luma needs a multiple of 32 lines, or chroma starts
  in the middle of a block and the end of the picture is written over it
- H.264: motion data for 17 pictures, kept between pictures (one 512 KB buffer before: too small above
  720p, and lost); a slot for each reference picture; direct_8x8_inference_flag; all 16 references
- MPEG-2: the quantiser matrices come in zigzag order, NVDEC takes them line by line
H.264 is now identical to FFmpeg's software decoder (baseline, high, 16 references, slices,
interlaced, up to 4K), MPEG-2 within the rounding of the IDCT (70 dB).

Read back: images in cached memory (dma-buf heap) instead of write-combined, kept for the next image.
vaDeriveImage is refused: surfaces are block linear.

Also: tegra-drm's render node is found by name, vaCreateContext fails when NVDEC cannot work (the player
then decodes with the CPU), objects are freed, engines are locked, the exported modifier is the real
one, quieter. On Tegra210 NVDEC is refused when the device tree does not power NVJPG with it:
the first access would stop the whole system.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
